# {{project_name}}

{{project_description}}

This is a local stdio Model Context Protocol server built with the stable TypeScript SDK v2. The protocol layer is deliberately thin: tool modules own MCP schemas and adaptation, while reusable behavior lives in ordinary TypeScript services.

## Requirements

- Node.js {{node_version}}+
- npm
- [just](https://github.com/casey/just)
- [Semgrep](https://semgrep.dev/)
- [codespell](https://github.com/codespell-project/codespell)

## Start

```bash
just init
just test
just run
```

`just run` starts a stdio server and waits for an MCP client. stdout is reserved for JSON-RPC protocol messages; application diagnostics are written to stderr.

To exercise tools interactively:

```bash
just inspect
```

## Architecture

```text
MCP stdio transport
        │
        ▼
src/server.ts
        │
        ▼
src/tools/*
        │
        ▼
src/services/*
```

- `src/index.ts` owns process startup and stdio lifecycle only.
- `src/server.ts` creates an `McpServer` and wires dependencies.
- `src/tools/` owns MCP input/output schemas and model-readable errors.
- `src/services/` owns transport-independent business behavior.
- `test/tools/` drives the server through a real MCP client, including the built stdio entrypoint.
- `test/services/` tests business behavior without MCP infrastructure.

One tool file should contain one MCP schema and adapter. Add resources, prompts, HTTP, or authentication only when the server actually needs them.

## Available tools

- `search` searches the example catalog. Both `query` and `limit` are required; omitted inputs are rejected instead of silently defaulted.
- `get-item` returns one item by exact identifier or a model-readable tool error.

Replace the example item service and tools with your domain behavior while preserving the protocol/service boundary.

## Host configuration

Build the server first:

```bash
just build
```

Then configure an MCP host to launch:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "{{project_name}}": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/{{project_name}}/dist/index.js"]
    }
  }
}
```

Use an absolute path in host configuration so launch behavior does not depend on the host's working directory.

## Validation

`just ci` runs initialization, formatting, linting, spelling, Semgrep guardrails, strict TypeScript checks, security linting, dependency hygiene, architecture checks, production build, real-client protocol tests, coverage thresholds, and dependency auditing. `just ci-quiet` runs the same checks with compact fail-fast output and is installed as the repository pre-commit hook.

Run `just help` for every available command.
